<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.1.xsd">
    <changeSet author="fineract" id="command-00006" context="postgresql,mysql">
        <comment>
            What an idempotency key is unique together with, as the configured distinctor computes it when the key is
            claimed. The empty string means nothing distinguishes the key, which is what every existing row gets and what
            a command without a key is stored with: not null, so that the unique constraint below compares two empty
            values as equal rather than treating two nulls as distinct.
        </comment>
        <addColumn tableName="m_command">
            <column name="custom_idempotency_distinctor" type="varchar(255)" defaultValue="">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>
    <changeSet author="fineract" id="command-00007" context="postgresql,mysql">
        <comment>
            Make idempotency keys unique, together with their distinctor, without losing any audit history. Where several rows share a key,
            the earliest one keeps it and the surplus rows get their row ID appended, so the original key
            stays readable and the record survives. No command record is deleted.

            Two details are deliberate: the rows are selected by ID and wrapped in a derived table because
            MySQL cannot reference the UPDATE target table directly in a subquery (error 1093), and the
            existing key is truncated before the suffix is appended so the result still fits varchar(255).
        </comment>
        <update tableName="m_command">
            <column name="idempotency_key" valueComputed="concat(left(idempotency_key, 200), '-duplicate-', id)"/>
            <where>
                id IN (
                    SELECT dup.id
                    FROM (
                        SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY idempotency_key ORDER BY id) AS dup_rank
                        FROM m_command
                        WHERE idempotency_key IS NOT NULL
                    ) dup
                    WHERE dup.dup_rank &gt; 1
                )
            </where>
        </update>
        <!--
            The non-unique index from 0002 is deliberately left in place rather than dropped and replaced. Dropping it
            would mean the rollback had to build it again, and Liquibase cannot create an index concurrently, so a
            rollback - which happens when something is already wrong - would lock m_command against reads and writes
            while it rebuilt. Leaving it means the rollback is a single constraint drop that returns the schema to
            exactly what it was. The cost is one redundant index on idempotency_key until it is dropped in a later
            release, which is the order this project uses for removals anyway.
        -->
        <addUniqueConstraint
                tableName="m_command"
                columnNames="idempotency_key, custom_idempotency_distinctor"
                constraintName="m_command_idempotency_key_distinctor_unique"/>
        <rollback>
            <!--
                Only the constraint is reverted. The suffixed keys are left alone: every row is still there,
                and re-applying this changeset over them is a no-op because they are already unique.
            -->
            <dropUniqueConstraint tableName="m_command" constraintName="m_command_idempotency_key_distinctor_unique"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
